1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,331
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,381 --> 00:00:02,366
Key Terms: Bias.

3
00:00:02,416 --> 00:00:07,198
Thirty-three terms for saying where bias
enters an AI pipeline, what a fix can

4
00:00:07,248 --> 00:00:09,614
actually reach, and who owns that fix.

5
00:00:09,664 --> 00:00:13,410
Useful because the word biased, on its
own, cannot be tested.

6
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:14,761
Hallo.

7
00:00:14,811 --> 00:00:16,267
This is Liam, in for Bear.

8
00:00:16,317 --> 00:00:19,279
People say that to fix a biased model, you
fix the model.

9
00:00:19,329 --> 00:00:23,043
Usually the bias came in somewhere else,
in who was sampled, how labels were made,

10
00:00:23,093 --> 00:00:25,051
or what a reviewer does with the score.

11
00:00:25,101 --> 00:00:26,808
So first find where the bias enters.

12
00:00:26,858 --> 00:00:29,820
This film goes through thirty-three terms
for doing that, each one shown.

13
00:00:30,170 --> 00:00:33,256
Five labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

14
00:00:33,306 --> 00:00:36,677
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

15
00:00:36,727 --> 00:00:39,812
Computed: the numbers are worked out on
screen from stated inputs.

16
00:00:39,862 --> 00:00:44,374
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

17
00:00:44,424 --> 00:00:47,224
Illustrative data: numbers chosen to show
the idea, not measured.

18
00:00:47,274 --> 00:00:50,360
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

19
00:00:50,710 --> 00:00:55,568
This is a map of the film, a table that
fills in one group at a time.

20
00:00:55,618 --> 00:00:59,610
Thirty-three terms, all about where bias
enters a pipeline, what a fix can reach,

21
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:00,765
and who owns it.

22
00:01:00,815 --> 00:01:02,786
The first group says what bias means.

23
00:01:02,836 --> 00:01:04,230
Estimator, bias and unbiased estimator.

24
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:07,268
The second group is ten ways in.

25
00:01:07,318 --> 00:01:11,570
Selection bias and sampling bias, with
inverse probability weighting as the

26
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:11,961
repair.

27
00:01:12,011 --> 00:01:16,263
Confirmation bias, self-report bias and
publication bias, with the funnel plot.

28
00:01:16,313 --> 00:01:19,783
Cohen's kappa, observer bias, data-coding
bias and data-entry bias.

29
00:01:19,833 --> 00:01:22,130
Then historical bias and implicit bias.

30
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:24,990
The third group asks where the bias sits.

31
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:26,910
Dataset bias sits in the sample.

32
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:29,790
Label bias and biased measurement sit in
the labels.

33
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:33,310
Structural bias sits in the world the
model is used in.

34
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:34,590
Sample, label or structure.

35
00:01:34,940 --> 00:01:38,000
The last group is causes and who decides.

36
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:40,721
Pearl's ladder, with association,
intervention and counterfactual.

37
00:01:40,771 --> 00:01:44,998
The causal graph, the proxy, leverage
analysis, leverage and the accountable

38
00:01:45,048 --> 00:01:45,386
owner.

39
00:01:45,436 --> 00:01:50,440
Then calibration parity, equal FPR and
FNR, the false positive and false negative

40
00:01:50,490 --> 00:01:53,550
rates, the impossibility theorem and the
values claim.

41
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:55,259
Here is a percent line.

42
00:01:55,309 --> 00:01:58,639
Each dot is one estimate from one sample,
in a simulated run.

43
00:01:58,689 --> 00:02:02,019
An estimator is the recipe that turns a
sample into a number.

44
00:02:02,069 --> 00:02:06,245
An unbiased estimator can miss on any one
sample, but its dots gather around the

45
00:02:06,295 --> 00:02:08,499
truth, the red dashed line at twenty-five
percent.

46
00:02:08,549 --> 00:02:12,724
For example, an agent averages the pay in
a random batch of data analyst listings.

47
00:02:12,774 --> 00:02:14,133
That average is an estimator.

48
00:02:14,183 --> 00:02:18,640
One batch may miss, but the batches center
on the true pay, so it is unbiased.

49
00:02:18,990 --> 00:02:22,932
Now red dots, from a sample that kept only
patients who passed a filter.

50
00:02:22,982 --> 00:02:24,928
On average it lands near fourteen percent.

51
00:02:24,978 --> 00:02:29,490
Bias is that offset from the truth, minus
ten point seven points, not the scatter of

52
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:30,060
the dots.

53
00:02:30,110 --> 00:02:34,052
For example, an agent estimates starting
pay only from offers people chose to post

54
00:02:34,102 --> 00:02:34,337
online.

55
00:02:34,387 --> 00:02:38,044
If people with good offers post more
often, the estimates center too high,

56
00:02:38,094 --> 00:02:38,899
batch after batch.

57
00:02:38,949 --> 00:02:40,610
That steady gap is the bias.

58
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:43,371
Now each sample grows to two thousand.

59
00:02:43,421 --> 00:02:46,886
Both clouds tighten, the red one around
the wrong value.

60
00:02:46,936 --> 00:02:48,995
More data shrinks scatter, not bias.

61
00:02:49,045 --> 00:02:53,214
The nineteen thirty-six Literary Digest
poll had two point four million responses

62
00:02:53,264 --> 00:02:54,620
and was still wrong.

63
00:02:54,970 --> 00:02:59,491
A thousand made-up patients and a six hour
lab filter that drops most of the critical

64
00:02:59,541 --> 00:02:59,776
ones.

65
00:02:59,826 --> 00:03:03,205
Selection bias means who gets in depends
on what happened to them.

66
00:03:03,255 --> 00:03:06,347
The kept sample shows fourteen point three
percent risk, not twenty-five.

67
00:03:06,397 --> 00:03:09,489
Picture a résumé tool that surveys only
the users still subscribed.

68
00:03:09,539 --> 00:03:12,632
Users who got no interviews quit early, so
they never answer.

69
00:03:12,682 --> 00:03:16,060
Who is in the survey depends on how their
job hunt went.

70
00:03:16,410 --> 00:03:17,565
Now a made-up survey.

71
00:03:17,615 --> 00:03:20,877
Professionals are forty percent of the
population but eighty percent of

72
00:03:20,927 --> 00:03:21,178
respondents.

73
00:03:21,228 --> 00:03:25,093
That is sampling bias, groups missed
because they were hard to reach, not

74
00:03:25,143 --> 00:03:26,298
because of their outcomes.

75
00:03:26,348 --> 00:03:28,406
Reweighting moves sixty-two percent back
to forty-six.

76
00:03:28,456 --> 00:03:32,019
For example, a job-market report surveys
seekers only through one career website.

77
00:03:32,069 --> 00:03:36,537
People who never use that site are hard to
reach, so they barely appear, however

78
00:03:36,587 --> 00:03:37,440
their search went.

79
00:03:37,790 --> 00:03:39,241
This is inverse probability weighting.

80
00:03:39,291 --> 00:03:43,745
Weight each kept patient by one over the
chance of being kept, so sixty critical

81
00:03:43,795 --> 00:03:45,547
patients stand in for three hundred.

82
00:03:45,597 --> 00:03:48,850
The estimate returns to twenty-five
percent, provided the cause of exclusion

83
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:49,450
was recorded.

84
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:54,254
For example, say the résumé tool recorded
how likely each kind of user was to keep

85
00:03:54,304 --> 00:03:54,555
answering.

86
00:03:54,605 --> 00:03:58,758
A user with no interviews who still
replied gets a large weight, standing in

87
00:03:58,808 --> 00:04:00,860
for those like her who went silent.

88
00:04:01,210 --> 00:04:05,128
A chart of belief over ten made-up pieces
of evidence, five for, five against.

89
00:04:05,178 --> 00:04:07,112
Weighed fairly, belief ends at fifty
percent.

90
00:04:07,162 --> 00:04:11,080
Let a prior count flattering pieces more
and the red line ends at ninety-seven.

91
00:04:11,130 --> 00:04:12,214
That is confirmation bias.

92
00:04:12,264 --> 00:04:16,182
For example, a developer sure her fit-
scoring agent works counts each good match

93
00:04:16,232 --> 00:04:16,748
as proof.

94
00:04:16,798 --> 00:04:21,567
Each bad match she sets aside as an odd
listing, so only the good ones reach her

95
00:04:21,617 --> 00:04:21,850
report.

96
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:23,406
Next, a made-up poll.

97
00:04:23,456 --> 00:04:25,917
One hundred people are asked whether they
voted.

98
00:04:25,967 --> 00:04:27,800
Sixty did, seventy-five say they did.

99
00:04:27,850 --> 00:04:31,253
That is self-report bias, people misreport
themselves in one consistent direction,

100
00:04:31,303 --> 00:04:32,194
toward looking good.

101
00:04:32,244 --> 00:04:34,705
Asking more people does not average it
away.

102
00:04:34,755 --> 00:04:38,472
For example, a job application asks
candidates to rate their own spreadsheet

103
00:04:38,522 --> 00:04:38,786
skills.

104
00:04:38,836 --> 00:04:43,180
Most round up and almost nobody rounds
down, so the error leans one way.

105
00:04:43,530 --> 00:04:46,529
Now eighty simulated studies, true effect
zero point one five.

106
00:04:46,579 --> 00:04:48,968
Filled dots were published, hollow ones
were not.

107
00:04:49,018 --> 00:04:50,187
That is publication bias.

108
00:04:50,237 --> 00:04:53,845
Significant results get published, nulls
stay in drawers, and the published pool

109
00:04:53,895 --> 00:04:55,674
alone shows zero point two one.

110
00:04:55,724 --> 00:04:59,638
For example, studies of AI résumé tools
get published when the tool raised

111
00:04:59,688 --> 00:04:59,942
callbacks.

112
00:04:59,992 --> 00:05:04,515
Studies where it made no difference stay
in a drawer, so the tools look stronger

113
00:05:04,565 --> 00:05:05,430
than they are.

114
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:09,748
Remove the unpublished studies and this is
a funnel plot, effect size against

115
00:05:09,798 --> 00:05:10,057
precision.

116
00:05:10,107 --> 00:05:14,384
It should be symmetric, but the lower left
corner, small null studies, is empty.

117
00:05:14,434 --> 00:05:18,092
Trim-and-fill adds seven stand-ins, moving
the pool to zero point one nine.

118
00:05:18,142 --> 00:05:22,110
For example, plot every published test of
whether a tailored cover letter brings

119
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:22,728
more callbacks.

120
00:05:22,778 --> 00:05:27,364
If the small tests that found nothing
never came out, the lower left of the

121
00:05:27,414 --> 00:05:28,600
funnel plot sits empty.

122
00:05:28,950 --> 00:05:30,342
This is an agreement table.

123
00:05:30,392 --> 00:05:34,379
Two blind coders label one hundred notes,
with made-up counts, and agree on eighty.

124
00:05:34,429 --> 00:05:36,974
Cohen's kappa asks how much of that beats
chance.

125
00:05:37,024 --> 00:05:40,434
Chance alone gives zero point five, so
kappa is zero point six.

126
00:05:40,484 --> 00:05:44,183
For example, two recruiters each mark the
same résumés as qualified or not.

127
00:05:44,233 --> 00:05:48,220
Kappa asks how far their agreement goes
beyond what two people guessing at their

128
00:05:48,270 --> 00:05:49,950
own rates would reach by luck.

129
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:54,448
Coder B can see each patient's prior
diagnosis, and kappa falls from zero point

130
00:05:54,498 --> 00:05:55,947
six to zero point two.

131
00:05:55,997 --> 00:05:58,946
A kappa that collapses on unblinding
points to observer bias.

132
00:05:58,996 --> 00:06:02,244
The labeler's prior belief is doing the
labeling, not the note.

133
00:06:02,294 --> 00:06:06,142
For example, a recruiter rating interview
notes can see each candidate's school, and

134
00:06:06,192 --> 00:06:08,241
rates notes from famous schools as
stronger.

135
00:06:08,291 --> 00:06:10,940
The school, not the notes, is setting the
label.

136
00:06:11,290 --> 00:06:14,574
Grids of who disagrees with the majority,
items down, coders across.

137
00:06:14,624 --> 00:06:16,695
Left, marks pile up on two items.

138
00:06:16,745 --> 00:06:20,029
That is data-coding bias, a vague
category, so revise the manual.

139
00:06:20,079 --> 00:06:22,151
Right, they pile up on one coder.

140
00:06:22,201 --> 00:06:23,363
That is observer bias.

141
00:06:23,413 --> 00:06:27,000
For example, taggers sorting job listings
into remote, hybrid or on-site disagree

142
00:06:27,050 --> 00:06:29,122
mostly on listings that say remote
friendly.

143
00:06:29,172 --> 00:06:34,274
The category is vague, not the taggers, so
that is data-coding bias, and the fix is a

144
00:06:34,324 --> 00:06:34,880
clearer rule.

145
00:06:35,230 --> 00:06:37,185
One hundred diagnoses, true code against
recorded.

146
00:06:37,235 --> 00:06:40,050
Twelve of thirty true C cases were entered
as B.

147
00:06:40,100 --> 00:06:42,915
That is data-entry bias, a patterned
error, not random noise.

148
00:06:42,965 --> 00:06:46,640
The right code sat three menus deep, so
the nearest one was entered.

149
00:06:46,690 --> 00:06:50,364
For example, an agent filling in
applications meets a long dropdown of job

150
00:06:50,414 --> 00:06:50,651
titles.

151
00:06:50,701 --> 00:06:55,521
When the right title sits far down the
list, it keeps picking a near match at the

152
00:06:55,571 --> 00:06:55,808
top.

153
00:06:55,858 --> 00:06:57,240
The error follows the form.

154
00:06:57,590 --> 00:06:59,816
Made-up promotion data beside a model's
predictions.

155
00:06:59,866 --> 00:07:03,068
Men thirty percent, women eighteen, and
the model matches both.

156
00:07:03,118 --> 00:07:04,369
That is historical bias.

157
00:07:04,419 --> 00:07:06,645
Unlike selection bias, nobody was filtered
out.

158
00:07:06,695 --> 00:07:10,548
The data faithfully copies an unfair past,
and accuracy is the harm.

159
00:07:10,598 --> 00:07:15,101
For example, a model that predicts who
gets an interview learns from a firm's

160
00:07:15,151 --> 00:07:16,401
complete, correct callback records.

161
00:07:16,451 --> 00:07:20,954
Those records show people with gaps in
their résumés were rarely called, and the

162
00:07:21,004 --> 00:07:22,580
model copies that past exactly.

163
00:07:22,930 --> 00:07:25,728
One box at the bottom feeds four boxes
above.

164
00:07:25,778 --> 00:07:29,209
The bottom box is implicit bias,
associations people hold without noticing.

165
00:07:29,259 --> 00:07:33,006
It surfaces as confirmation, observer,
data coding and publication bias, so there

166
00:07:33,056 --> 00:07:34,905
is no single point to fix.

167
00:07:34,955 --> 00:07:38,702
For example, a hiring manager warms,
without noticing, to candidates who share

168
00:07:38,752 --> 00:07:39,335
her background.

169
00:07:39,385 --> 00:07:43,765
That same pull shapes which résumés she
opens first, how she hears their answers,

170
00:07:43,815 --> 00:07:45,980
and which hires she reports as successes.

171
00:07:46,330 --> 00:07:47,516
Now a parity audit.

172
00:07:47,566 --> 00:07:51,533
One group is predicted positive forty
percent of the time, the other twenty-

173
00:07:51,583 --> 00:07:51,842
five.

174
00:07:51,892 --> 00:07:54,314
That gap is a flag, not a verdict.

175
00:07:54,364 --> 00:07:58,022
Pipeline bias and a real base-rate
difference between the groups show the

176
00:07:58,072 --> 00:07:58,640
same gap.

177
00:07:58,990 --> 00:08:00,383
Stacked bars, with made-up shares.

178
00:08:00,433 --> 00:08:02,692
The training set is ninety percent group
one.

179
00:08:02,742 --> 00:08:05,578
The people the model is used on are split
evenly.

180
00:08:05,628 --> 00:08:07,887
That mismatch is dataset bias, a sampling
problem.

181
00:08:07,937 --> 00:08:10,484
Fix the sample and, in principle, the bias
goes.

182
00:08:10,534 --> 00:08:13,948
For example, a fit-scoring agent is
trained mostly on résumés for software

183
00:08:13,998 --> 00:08:16,545
jobs, then used to score nurses and
teachers too.

184
00:08:16,595 --> 00:08:19,720
The people it learned from are not the
people it scores.

185
00:08:20,070 --> 00:08:20,830
Now the label.

186
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:25,420
Both areas have the same true crime rate,
but detection is twice as high in one, so

187
00:08:25,470 --> 00:08:27,040
its recorded re-arrest rate is double.

188
00:08:27,090 --> 00:08:29,740
Re-arrest is a biased measurement, a
distorted stand-in for crime.

189
00:08:29,790 --> 00:08:31,900
Train on it and you get label bias.

190
00:08:31,950 --> 00:08:35,680
For example, a hiring model learns what a
good hire is from managers' first-year

191
00:08:35,730 --> 00:08:35,950
ratings.

192
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,730
If those ratings mark some people down,
the rating is a biased measurement, and

193
00:08:39,780 --> 00:08:41,890
the model learns the rating, not the work.

194
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:44,780
The model predicts who succeeds, and it is
right.

195
00:08:44,830 --> 00:08:47,945
But group one had eighty percent mentor
access, group two forty.

196
00:08:47,995 --> 00:08:49,096
That is structural bias.

197
00:08:49,146 --> 00:08:51,973
Data, labels and model are fine, the world
is unequal.

198
00:08:52,023 --> 00:08:54,275
Retraining leaves the twenty point gap in
success.

199
00:08:54,325 --> 00:08:57,728
For example, an agent correctly predicts
that applicants with referrals get hired

200
00:08:57,778 --> 00:08:58,303
more often.

201
00:08:58,353 --> 00:09:02,331
But referrals depend on who you already
know, and that is not spread evenly.

202
00:09:02,381 --> 00:09:03,770
No model fix touches it.

203
00:09:04,120 --> 00:09:07,626
This is Pearl's ladder, three levels of
causal reasoning drawn as rungs.

204
00:09:07,676 --> 00:09:10,293
Seeing, the bottom rung, asks what goes
with what.

205
00:09:10,343 --> 00:09:13,849
Doing, the middle rung, asks what happens
if we set something ourselves.

206
00:09:13,899 --> 00:09:17,109
Imagining, the top rung, is the
counterfactual, what would have happened

207
00:09:17,159 --> 00:09:19,776
to this one case if something had been
different.

208
00:09:19,826 --> 00:09:23,332
For example, seeing notices that
applicants who send cover letters get more

209
00:09:23,382 --> 00:09:23,628
interviews.

210
00:09:23,678 --> 00:09:26,591
Doing adds letters to a random half of the
applications.

211
00:09:26,641 --> 00:09:30,444
Imagining asks whether this job seeker
would have got this interview without her

212
00:09:30,494 --> 00:09:30,740
letter.

213
00:09:31,090 --> 00:09:34,779
Rung one is association, the chance of an
outcome given what we observe.

214
00:09:34,829 --> 00:09:38,806
In this made-up approval model, group A
gets forty-six percent and group B thirty-

215
00:09:38,856 --> 00:09:39,094
four.

216
00:09:39,144 --> 00:09:41,107
That twelve point gap is an observation.

217
00:09:41,157 --> 00:09:42,833
It cannot say what causes it.

218
00:09:42,883 --> 00:09:46,573
For example, job seekers who use an AI-
tailored résumé get more interviews.

219
00:09:46,623 --> 00:09:47,435
That is association.

220
00:09:47,485 --> 00:09:51,750
The tool might help, or the people who use
it might already apply more carefully.

221
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:54,802
Rung two is intervention, setting a
variable, not watching it.

222
00:09:54,852 --> 00:09:59,480
Set the zip code score to a neutral value,
run the model again, and the gap falls

223
00:09:59,530 --> 00:10:00,856
from twelve points to two.

224
00:10:00,906 --> 00:10:03,058
Most of it ran through the zip code.

225
00:10:03,108 --> 00:10:06,911
For example, a job-search agent tailors
the résumé for a random half of its

226
00:10:06,961 --> 00:10:09,388
listings and sends the plain one to the
rest.

227
00:10:09,438 --> 00:10:12,690
Setting the variable by coin flip, not
watching who tailors, is intervention.

228
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:14,674
This diagram is a causal graph.

229
00:10:14,724 --> 00:10:18,603
Boxes are variables, arrows run from cause
to effect, from the protected attribute to

230
00:10:18,653 --> 00:10:19,164
the outcome.

231
00:10:19,214 --> 00:10:23,654
The red box is a proxy, a feature that
tracks the attribute, such as zip code.

232
00:10:23,704 --> 00:10:27,022
The lower route goes through a human
reviewer and skips the model.

233
00:10:27,072 --> 00:10:30,951
For example, in a résumé screener's graph,
graduation year is a proxy for age.

234
00:10:31,001 --> 00:10:34,880
The model never sees age, but age still
reaches the score through that arrow.

235
00:10:35,230 --> 00:10:39,066
Leverage analysis lists every path from
attribute to outcome and asks how much

236
00:10:39,116 --> 00:10:39,963
bias each carries.

237
00:10:40,013 --> 00:10:42,354
Line width is the load, with made-up
numbers.

238
00:10:42,404 --> 00:10:46,838
Of fifty points, the model paths carry two
and three, the thick red reviewer path

239
00:10:46,888 --> 00:10:47,137
forty-five.

240
00:10:47,187 --> 00:10:50,725
For example, a hiring gap could run
through who applies, through the

241
00:10:50,775 --> 00:10:53,714
screener's score, or through the recruiter
who works the shortlist.

242
00:10:53,764 --> 00:10:57,600
Leverage analysis measures how much each
route carries before anyone picks a fix.

243
00:10:57,950 --> 00:11:00,836
Leverage is the share of that bias one
action stops.

244
00:11:00,886 --> 00:11:02,598
Each red cross is a fix.

245
00:11:02,648 --> 00:11:04,359
Repairing the sample blocks four percent.

246
00:11:04,409 --> 00:11:05,828
A fairness penalty blocks six.

247
00:11:05,878 --> 00:11:07,296
Changing the review blocks ninety.

248
00:11:07,346 --> 00:11:09,057
That is the high leverage point.

249
00:11:09,107 --> 00:11:13,168
For example, retraining a résumé screener
might block a sliver of a hiring gap,

250
00:11:13,218 --> 00:11:16,985
while changing how recruiters break ties
on the shortlist blocks most of it.

251
00:11:17,035 --> 00:11:19,040
The tie-break change has the higher
leverage.

252
00:11:19,390 --> 00:11:20,808
A node has an owner.

253
00:11:20,858 --> 00:11:24,626
The accountable owner is whoever controls
the high leverage node, here whoever runs

254
00:11:24,676 --> 00:11:25,507
the review process.

255
00:11:25,557 --> 00:11:26,975
Naming them is not blame.

256
00:11:27,025 --> 00:11:31,086
Leverage analysis finds the path, and the
accountable owner is who can change it.

257
00:11:31,136 --> 00:11:35,491
For example, if most of a hiring gap runs
through the final interview panel, the

258
00:11:35,541 --> 00:11:39,603
accountable owner is whoever sets how that
panel decides, not the engineer who built

259
00:11:39,653 --> 00:11:40,190
the screener.

260
00:11:40,540 --> 00:11:43,858
These charts show different ideas of fair,
with made-up rates.

261
00:11:43,908 --> 00:11:48,909
Calibration parity says a score means the
same in every group, here sixty percent of

262
00:11:48,959 --> 00:11:51,267
those flagged are truly positive in both.

263
00:11:51,317 --> 00:11:55,982
Equal FPR and FNR, false positive rate and
false negative rate, says errors fall

264
00:11:56,032 --> 00:11:56,319
equally.

265
00:11:56,369 --> 00:11:59,350
For example, take a screener that marks
applicants qualified.

266
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:04,064
Calibration parity means those it marks
turn out qualified just as often in every

267
00:12:04,114 --> 00:12:04,401
group.

268
00:12:04,451 --> 00:12:08,779
Equal FPR and FNR means unqualified people
passed, and qualified people rejected, at

269
00:12:08,829 --> 00:12:10,800
the same rates in every group.

270
00:12:11,150 --> 00:12:11,999
Now the catch.

271
00:12:12,049 --> 00:12:15,596
Each grid is one hundred people, filled
dots are the truly positive.

272
00:12:15,646 --> 00:12:20,093
That share is the base rate, thirty
percent in group one, fifty in group two.

273
00:12:20,143 --> 00:12:23,090
Unequal base rates set the fairness
measures against each other.

274
00:12:23,440 --> 00:12:27,169
The impossibility theorem says that with
unequal base rates, calibration parity,

275
00:12:27,219 --> 00:12:29,917
equal FPR and equal FNR cannot all hold.

276
00:12:29,967 --> 00:12:34,726
In table A, TPR, the true positive rate,
is equal, so PPV, positive predictive

277
00:12:34,776 --> 00:12:35,413
value, breaks.

278
00:12:35,463 --> 00:12:38,504
In table B, PPV is equal, so TPR breaks.

279
00:12:38,554 --> 00:12:42,626
For example, if more of one group's
applicants are qualified, a screener

280
00:12:42,676 --> 00:12:48,466
cannot make its yes mean the same in both
groups and also make both kinds of mistake

281
00:12:48,516 --> 00:12:49,840
equally often in both.

282
00:12:50,190 --> 00:12:51,477
Someone absorbs the error.

283
00:12:51,527 --> 00:12:55,486
Option A gives group one more wrong flags,
forty percent against twenty-two.

284
00:12:55,536 --> 00:12:59,162
Option B misses more of group two's
positives, eighty-five percent against

285
00:12:59,212 --> 00:12:59,496
sixty-five.

286
00:12:59,546 --> 00:13:01,166
No arithmetic picks between them.

287
00:13:01,216 --> 00:13:03,505
Choosing the metric is a values claim.

288
00:13:03,555 --> 00:13:07,181
For example, a company picking its
screener's metric is choosing between

289
00:13:07,231 --> 00:13:10,856
rejecting more qualified people in one
group or interviewing more unqualified

290
00:13:10,906 --> 00:13:12,193
people in the other.

291
00:13:12,243 --> 00:13:15,200
That is a decision about who bears the
error.

292
00:13:15,550 --> 00:13:18,306
Now pairs that get mixed up, side by side.

293
00:13:18,356 --> 00:13:19,241
Left, selection bias.

294
00:13:19,291 --> 00:13:23,917
A filter drops people, here the sickest
dots fade out, so the sample misses them.

295
00:13:23,967 --> 00:13:24,852
Right, historical bias.

296
00:13:24,902 --> 00:13:27,346
Nobody is dropped and every label is
accurate.

297
00:13:27,396 --> 00:13:29,216
The data shows an unfair world.

298
00:13:29,266 --> 00:13:31,710
The test is whether anyone was filtered
out.

299
00:13:32,060 --> 00:13:32,568
Second pair.

300
00:13:32,618 --> 00:13:33,126
Left, association.

301
00:13:33,176 --> 00:13:37,032
You watch X, and Y rises with it, but you
cannot tell the reason.

302
00:13:37,082 --> 00:13:37,590
Right, intervention.

303
00:13:37,640 --> 00:13:40,379
You set X by hand and see whether Y moves.

304
00:13:40,429 --> 00:13:43,169
Here it does not, so X was not the cause.

305
00:13:43,219 --> 00:13:45,680
Ask whether anyone set X, or only watched
it.

306
00:13:46,030 --> 00:13:47,787
Third pair, with made-up rates.

307
00:13:47,837 --> 00:13:48,871
Left, calibration parity.

308
00:13:48,921 --> 00:13:55,015
PPV, the share of flags that are right, is
sixty percent in both groups, but TPR is

309
00:13:55,065 --> 00:13:56,099
thirty-five against fifteen.

310
00:13:56,149 --> 00:14:00,436
Right, equal error rates, the same idea as
equal FPR and FNR.

311
00:14:00,486 --> 00:14:04,050
TPR matches, and PPV splits, sixty against
seventy-seven point eight.

312
00:14:04,400 --> 00:14:04,986
Last pair.

313
00:14:05,036 --> 00:14:07,847
Left, dataset bias, the wrong people in
the sample.

314
00:14:07,897 --> 00:14:10,708
Training is mostly group one, deployment
is split evenly.

315
00:14:10,758 --> 00:14:13,252
Right, label bias, the wrong thing written
down.

316
00:14:13,302 --> 00:14:16,431
The true rate is twenty percent, the
recorded rate ten.

317
00:14:16,481 --> 00:14:19,610
One is a sampling problem, the other a
measurement problem.

318
00:14:19,960 --> 00:14:21,600
Here vague claims become exact.

319
00:14:21,650 --> 00:14:23,289
First, the model is biased.

320
00:14:23,339 --> 00:14:25,317
Name the target and the estimate.

321
00:14:25,367 --> 00:14:29,711
In the made-up patient example, the sample
says fourteen point three percent, the

322
00:14:29,761 --> 00:14:33,090
truth is twenty-five, the bias minus ten
point seven points.

323
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:35,231
Second, just collect more data.

324
00:14:35,281 --> 00:14:37,440
More data narrows scatter, not offset.

325
00:14:37,490 --> 00:14:39,281
SD, standard deviation, measures scatter.

326
00:14:39,331 --> 00:14:45,173
It shrinks from five points to zero point
five as samples grow from fifty to five

327
00:14:45,223 --> 00:14:45,541
thousand.

328
00:14:45,591 --> 00:14:47,750
The red bias bar never changes.

329
00:14:48,100 --> 00:14:49,715
Third, the model is fair.

330
00:14:49,765 --> 00:14:51,048
Ask by which metric.

331
00:14:51,098 --> 00:14:55,378
Here TPR and FPR are equal across groups,
thirty-five percent and ten percent.

332
00:14:55,428 --> 00:15:00,041
Yet PPV is sixty percent in one group,
seventy-seven point eight in the other.

333
00:15:00,091 --> 00:15:01,040
Name the metric.

334
00:15:01,390 --> 00:15:03,103
Fourth, check whether it is causal.

335
00:15:03,153 --> 00:15:04,865
The exact version is an intervention.

336
00:15:04,915 --> 00:15:08,978
Set the zip code proxy to a neutral value
and run the model again.

337
00:15:09,028 --> 00:15:11,328
The gap of twelve points drops to two.

338
00:15:11,378 --> 00:15:13,090
The proxy carried most of it.

339
00:15:13,440 --> 00:15:14,661
Fifth, fix the bias.

340
00:15:14,711 --> 00:15:16,885
The exact version is a leverage analysis.

341
00:15:16,935 --> 00:15:21,016
Draw the causal graph, list the three
paths, block the one carrying forty-five

342
00:15:21,066 --> 00:15:23,558
of fifty points, and name its accountable
owner.

343
00:15:23,608 --> 00:15:26,100
It is rarely the fix nearest your hands.

344
00:15:26,450 --> 00:15:29,766
A checklist to run before accepting any
bias claim or fix.

345
00:15:29,816 --> 00:15:34,050
First, ask which number is the estimate,
and what the target is, written theta.

346
00:15:34,100 --> 00:15:35,886
Bias is the gap between them.

347
00:15:35,936 --> 00:15:38,640
If nobody can name both, nothing can be
tested.

348
00:15:38,990 --> 00:15:39,515
Second check.

349
00:15:39,565 --> 00:15:42,678
Ask whether who got in depended on what
happened to them.

350
00:15:42,728 --> 00:15:46,990
If yes, that is selection bias, and more
data from the same filter sharpens the

351
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:47,565
wrong answer.

352
00:15:47,615 --> 00:15:50,440
If groups were hard to reach, it is
sampling bias.

353
00:15:50,790 --> 00:15:51,331
Third check.

354
00:15:51,381 --> 00:15:56,352
Ask whether the data is a faithful picture
of a world you did not want to copy.

355
00:15:56,402 --> 00:15:57,534
That is historical bias.

356
00:15:57,584 --> 00:16:00,783
Every quality check passes, because
nothing in the data is wrong.

357
00:16:00,833 --> 00:16:02,260
Here accuracy is the problem.

358
00:16:02,610 --> 00:16:03,149
Fourth check.

359
00:16:03,199 --> 00:16:04,623
Ask where the bias sits.

360
00:16:04,673 --> 00:16:06,686
In the sample, that is dataset bias.

361
00:16:06,736 --> 00:16:08,749
In the labels, that is label bias.

362
00:16:08,799 --> 00:16:12,286
In the world the model is deployed into,
that is structural bias.

363
00:16:12,336 --> 00:16:13,760
Each needs a different fix.

364
00:16:14,110 --> 00:16:14,766
Fifth check.

365
00:16:14,816 --> 00:16:16,531
Rung one or rung two.

366
00:16:16,581 --> 00:16:20,061
Ask whether anyone set the variable, or
only watched it.

367
00:16:20,111 --> 00:16:21,120
Watching gives association.

368
00:16:21,170 --> 00:16:22,179
Setting gives intervention.

369
00:16:22,229 --> 00:16:26,768
A fairness metric computed from observed
data is association, so it cannot settle

370
00:16:26,818 --> 00:16:28,180
what causes the gap.

371
00:16:28,530 --> 00:16:28,808
Last.

372
00:16:28,858 --> 00:16:32,744
Ask which path carries the most bias, and
who owns that node.

373
00:16:32,794 --> 00:16:35,368
That is leverage analysis and the
accountable owner.

374
00:16:35,418 --> 00:16:40,616
Then say it exactly, for example a bias of
minus ten point seven points against a

375
00:16:40,666 --> 00:16:41,600
twenty-five percent target.

376
00:16:42,450 --> 00:16:43,029
Let's recap.

377
00:16:43,079 --> 00:16:47,435
Bias is an offset from the truth, and more
data does not move it.

378
00:16:47,485 --> 00:16:50,582
Selection bias drops people, historical
bias copies an unfair world.

379
00:16:50,632 --> 00:16:54,044
Bias can sit in the sample, the labels or
the structure.

380
00:16:54,094 --> 00:16:56,247
Association watches a variable,
intervention sets it.

381
00:16:56,297 --> 00:16:59,394
Leverage analysis finds the path, the
accountable owner controls it.

382
00:16:59,444 --> 00:17:03,170
With unequal base rates, choosing a
fairness metric is a values claim.

383
00:17:03,520 --> 00:17:05,039
Quiz me on bias terms.

384
00:17:05,089 --> 00:17:10,061
Give me short cases, one at a time, about
a data pipeline or a deployed model.

385
00:17:10,111 --> 00:17:14,456
For each, I must name the exact term, such
as selection bias, sampling bias,

386
00:17:14,506 --> 00:17:17,281
historical bias, observer bias, label
bias, structural bias, association,

387
00:17:17,331 --> 00:17:20,733
intervention, leverage or accountable
owner, and say why its look-alike is

388
00:17:20,783 --> 00:17:21,047
wrong.

389
00:17:21,097 --> 00:17:23,558
Do not reveal the answer until I reply.

390
00:17:23,608 --> 00:17:24,500
Then correct me.

391
00:17:24,850 --> 00:17:26,141
Key Terms: Bias.

392
00:17:26,191 --> 00:17:27,930
At Nik Bear Brown.
